@NODE usedmem "ENVIRONLIB"

@{fg shine}Function: UsedMem@{fg text}
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Modes  : 
Syntax : UsedMem

Returns the amount of current allocated memory in bytes.
It tracks all memory movements done by memlib and all commands that make used of memlib.

Please note:
This command may not track *all* the memory that your application consumes, just the 
allocations that are done via memlib. Anyway, as most of the blitzlibs make use of 
memlib, UsedMem gets a good indication about the allocated memory.

If a blitzlib or your program allocated memory using os functions directly (e.g. AllocMem_(), FreeMem_()),
UsedMem will not track it.

Beware of the fact, that beside the allocated memory, there will sometimes be an amount of management data allocated.
For example when dimensioning an array, some memory must be allocated to hold the data to manage the array.

--> Dynamic List allocations are currently not tracked by this command !

@ENDNODE usedmem

@node "NTSC" "ENVIRONLIB"
 @{fg shine}Statement: NTSC@{fg text}
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Modes  :
Syntax : NTSC

This function returns 0 if the display is currently in PAL mode, or -1 if
currently in NTSC mode. This may be used to write software which dynamically
adjusts itself to different versions of the Amiga computer.

@endnode

@node "DispHeight" "ENVIRONLIB"
 @{fg shine}Statement: DispHeight@{fg text}
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Modes  :
Syntax : DispHeight

DispHeight will return 256 if executed on a PAL Amiga, or 200 if executed on
an NTSC Amiga. This allows programs to open full sized screens, windows etc
on any Amiga.

@endnode

@node "VPos" "ENVIRONLIB"
 @{fg shine}Statement: VPos@{fg text}
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Modes  :
Syntax : VPos

VPos returns the video's beam vertical position. Useful in both highspeed
animation where screen update may need to be synced to a certain video beam
position (not just the top of frame as with VWait) and for a fast random
nember generator in non frame-synced applications.

@endnode

@NODE "GetGlobalStrAddr"
Function: GetGlobalStrAddr
******************
     Syntax: n=GetGlobalStrAddr (String$)
      Modes: Amiga or Blitz
Description:
Many Amiga shared libraries (like the DOS library) require addresses
of null-terminated strings as arguments.  This function will return
a long-integer address of a null-terminated string in memory for such
commands.
Function named befor 'Null' to the change of the compiler it was needed
to rename it. For .bb2 format source it will converted automaticly but
for .ab2 and .ab3 format source it is needed to convert it by hand
from 'Null (String$)' to 'GetGlobalStrAddr (String$)'


>> This function is not needed anymore since BlitzBasic 2 because it provides
always null-terminated strings you can use '&' instead like n=&String$ !

@ENDNODE

@NODE "Null"
Function: NULL
******************
     Syntax: Null
      Modes: Amiga or Blitz
Description:
Will Return 0 in D0 but it will be overwritten be the compiler to 0 in Dx.
@ENDNODE


@NODE "ChipFree"
Function: CHIPFREE
******************
     Syntax: n=ChipFree
      Modes: Amiga or Blitz
Description:
This function will return the size, in bytes, of the largest block
of free CHIP memory in your system.
 See Also:
 FastFree
 LargestFree
@ENDNODE


@NODE "FastFree"
Function: FASTFREE
******************
     Syntax: n=FastFree
      Modes: Amiga or Blitz
Description:
This function returns the size of the largest block of FAST memory.
@ENDNODE


@NODE "LargestFree"
Function: LARGESTFREE
*********************
     Syntax: n=LargestFree
      Modes: Amiga or Blitz
Description:
This function will return the size of the largest chunk of memory
available.  This memory may be FAST or CHIP, depending on your system.
@ENDNODE


